= Carnaps quasi-analysis =
====== short ======
Carnaps technique of quasi-analysis is a key part of both Russell's attempted
reduction of mathematics to pure logic (rather than set theory) and
Wittgenstein's account of propositions and symbols in the tractatus.

====== related to ======
++[[russellian_semantics#denoting phrases meaningless in isolation]]
++[[tractatus]]

–[[dennett2017-historyOfQualia]] (qualia without Cartesian theatre)

[[local:zz.texts/carnap1926-derAufbauDerWelt.pdf]]!!!
(see annotations in pdf)

= main =
== technique ==
Carnap's initial description of “quasi-analysis” is cryptic:
    Generally speaking . . .  quasi analysis is to achieve the following:
    unanalyzable units of any kind, a pair list of which is presupposed, are to
    be manipulated with the constructional ascension forms of class and relation
    extension (i.e., with synthetic methods) in such a way that the result is a
    formal substitution for proper analysis (i.e., the analysis into
    constituents or properties), which cannot be carried out in this case.

Lets consider an example from Carnap:
Suppose we have a list of pairs of objects. In the cases we will be interested
in these will be a list of pairs of objects that are like each other in some
way. Each pair might have some property in common, but for whatever reason we
are prevented from producing an analysis of these object's properties. All we
know about these objects is which of pairs of them are on this list (i.e. we
know the extension of the relationship “similar-to”).

On this basis of this single relationship of “similar-to” we can define terms
that behave like ascriptions of properties that these objects have in common.
    Let there be altogether five different colors. Let us define the relation of
    "color kinship" in such a way that it is to hold for two things if these
    have at least one color in common. Let the things be individually
    designated, for example, by numbers. Now let us assume that we do not know
    of any of the things which colors it has. All we have is a pair list (i.e.,
    we know only the extension of the relation of color kinship: we are told all
    pairs for which this relation holds, but we are not told which color these
    two things have in common). In other words, the extension of the relation of
    color kinship is completely given. Now, our task consists in inferring from
    these data the distribution of colors. We cannot proceed by choosing one of
    the things at random and determining all its color kin on the basis of the
    pair list, for it does not follow that all these are color akin to each
    other.

For instance, one object _A_ might be both partly red (and hence
similar[/related by _colour kinship_] to other objects that are at least partly
red) and partly green (and hence similar[/related by _colour kinship_] to other
objects that are at least partly green).  But there may be two (or more) objects
that are colour kin to _A_ but not colour kin to each other (e.g. one object
that is purely green and another that is purely red).

On the basis of this similarity relation, provided it is symmetric(:anything I am similar to is similar to me:) and
reflexive(:each thing is similar to itself:) we can determine how many different colours there are, and identify
the extension of each colour (i.e. the classes of objects that are partly that
colour), though of course we cannot identify the colours themselves. We might,
for instance, determine that there are 5 colours and that one colour is shared
by objects A, B and C and another colour and B, C, D and E all share another
colour, but we cannot work out which colour is red or green etc., (Carnap has a
problem of free-loaders. If each partly blue thing is also partly red, the
extension of colour-kinship will not allow us to distinguish blue from red.
Wittgenstein does not have this problem since he is concerned with the space of
possible propositions.)

The two principles for assigning colours to objects (or rather objects to colour
classes) are:
    First of all, any two elements of a color class stand in the relation of
    color kinship to one another (because the members of the pair both have the
    color which determines the color class). Secondly, the color classes are the
    largest possible classes all of whose members are color-akin.
Carnap intends to use this technique to deliniate sensation types. Say, seeing a
red square in the lower right quadrant of the visual field, or hearing the tone
middle C.  But he cant find unproblematic sensory elements from which to
composes whole/gestalt sensations.  In particular he is worried that the hearing
of the chord composed of the notes C-E-G is _not_ a sensation that is composed
of hearing C, hearing E, and hearing G, but rather a sort of organic whole that
taken by itself has nothing tripartite about it. Rather than trying to identify
the property _Hearing-C(...)_, he instead simply identifies the set of sounds
which are tone kin to each other without postulating some phenomena which is
common to them all.

On the basis of the relation of colour similarity we can define a number of
(one-place) predicates such as _Colour1_, _Colour2_, in terms of the (two-place)
_colour kinship_ relationship.
The trick hinges on quasi-identifying the property with the class of things that
are related by the relevant similarity relation. Attributions of membership of
the relevant class of objects related by colour/tone kinship (or whatever the
relevant form of similarity is) can do the job of attributions of properties.
Instead of talking about the property of _being_ red, or some other constituent of
these objects that is supposed to be common between them, we can instead say
that they are members of a colour class. (i.e. a maximal class of objects that
are related to each other by the _similar to_ relation).

We identify (in a loose sense of identity) the common property with the class of
objects that are related to each other by similarity.  In short rather that
saying “P(a)” we say [a is a member of {x: x is a member of a largest set of
objects that all bear the similarity relationship to each other}].

== basis ==
*ontological commitments*
The basis of Carnap's logical constructions in [[local:zz.texts/carnap1926-derAufbauDerWelt.pdf|_der Aufbau der Welt_]]
are terms denoting fundamental irreducibly gestalt experiences. From this
foundation Carnap proposed to logically construct talk of all other objects
(phenomenal and physical).

These gestalt experiences stood to each other in relations of part-identity and
part-similarity but were not themselves composed of phenomenal entities (or
indeed of anything). In Carnap's system, coloured sections of the visual field,
for instance, are treated as classes of gestalt experiences (those which we may
afterwards identify as being coloured alike in that section).  Carnap intended
this to eliminate, in the final analysis, all objects (phenomenal and otherwise)
apart from the gestalt experiences themselves. Even reference to classes of
these fundamental total experiences was to be viewed merely as a way of talking
about the experiences themselves instead of distinct objects:

    The "class" is not, properly speaking, an object. Its symbol merely serves
    to make those assertions which hold equally for all its elements

We may be unconvinced that ontological commitments to classes can be so
trivially dispensed with. We may also remain unsure if the postulation of some
kind of experiential/phenomenological entities is indispensable to our talk of
psychological states. However this approach was a key commonality between
Wittgenstein, Carnap and Russell. It is particularly key to 
